## 1. PURPOSE

This script implements a high-performance computer vision system
for the Eurobot 2026 competition. The goal is to provide the robot
with reliable and robust perception of its environment, allowing it
to identify and locate target objects (“nuts”) with high precision.

## 2. RELIABILITY STRATEGY

To ensure near 100% reliability, our approach is built on three pillars:

  a) THE RIGHT TOOL: INSTANCE SEGMENTATION (YOLOv8-seg)
     Unlike bounding box detection, segmentation identifies the exact
     shape of an object at pixel level. This is crucial for distinguishing
     touching or partially occluded objects, eliminating a major source
     of detection errors.

  b) HIGH-QUALITY TRAINING
     An AI model performs well only if its training data is excellent.
     Our method consists of:
       - COLLECTING an initial dataset of 100–200 photos of the “nuts”
         under varied conditions (lighting, angle, distance, context).
       - PRECISELY ANNOTATING these images to teach the AI what a “nut” is.
       - AUGMENTING the dataset (using tools like Roboflow) to generate
         thousands of variations. This helps the AI generalize and remain
         robust in unseen situations.

  c) THE CONFIDENCE FILTER
     The AI computes a confidence score for each detection. The robot
     is programmed to IGNORE any detection below a high confidence threshold
     (e.g., 70–80%). This is the final safety net against false positives.

## 3. HOW THE SCRIPT WORKS (FOR DEMO)

This script uses a pre-trained YOLOv8 model on a large dataset (COCO).

  - When launched, an interactive window pops up.
  - The user can type the name of a known object (e.g., "bottle", "cup",
    "cell phone"). A dropdown list with autocomplete helps selection.
  - After validation, a single window appears, divided into two views:
    - LEFT (“Robot Brain”): a “Tunnel Vision” view. Everything is black
      except for target objects detected with high confidence. This is the
      robot’s “conscious” perception, focused only on its mission.
    - RIGHT (“Camera View”): the raw video feed, showing what the camera
      physically sees.

## 4. NEXT STEPS (CUSTOM TRAINING FOR “NUTS”)

The simplest way to train a custom model is to use an online platform
like Roboflow:

  1. TAKE PHOTOS: Capture 100+ images of the “nuts”.
  2. UPLOAD & ANNOTATE: Upload to Roboflow and draw masks.
  3. GENERATE DATASET: Apply augmentations (rotation, brightness…)
     to increase dataset size and diversity.
  4. TRAIN: Click “Train”. Roboflow handles the training in the cloud.
  5. USE THE MODEL: Once trained, replace
     `model = YOLO('yolov8n-seg.pt')` with your custom model path,
     e.g. `model = YOLO('runs/train/weights/best.pt')`.

This methodology ensures fast, iterative development and
competition-level performance for the robot’s perception system.
"""
